 To separate quota from eligible-source ordering under intermittent arrivals, two preregistered RTL campaigns cross High:Low quotas Q1 ($1:1$) and Q2 ($2:1$) with oldest-first (OF) and eligible-round-robin (RR) selection under bursty 90\% readiness (Figure~\ref{fig:followup-20261102}a, Table~\ref{tab:nov-ordering}). In the twenty-seed W256 confirmation (seeds 6501--6520, 160 RTL jobs, 80 mixed/isolated pairs), every seed crosses one active boundary while remaining effectively High. The Q1 ordering delta $d_1=\mathrm{S(Q1,RR)}-\mathrm{S(Q1,OF)}$ is zero in 20/20 seeds; the Q2 ordering delta $d_2$ and the quota-by-ordering interaction $I=d_2-d_1$ are $-1$ cycle in 7/20 seeds and zero in 13/20 (mean $-0.35$, median $0$). Because the frozen gate required at least 15/20 joint strict improvements, the primary superiority claim is \code{NOT\_CONFIRMED}. In the separate five-seed W5M production exploration (seeds 6601--6605, 40 RTL jobs, 20 pairs, 5M Heavy requests per job), every seed crosses the sole active boundary at cycle 4,999,999 ($H/H/H/H\rightarrow B_H/H/H/H$) and finishes near 6.25M cycles with matched Sparse cohorts and zero censoring. All five seeds yield mixed Sparse P99 of $S=11/11/12/11$ cycles across Q1-OF, Q1-RR, Q2-OF, and Q2-RR ($d_1=0$, $d_2=-1$, $I=-1$). However, RR increases the Q1 mixed Heavy maximum wall gap by $+2,+2,+1,+2,+1$ cycles (mean $+1.6$) while Q2 changes by $0,0,0,0,-1$ (mean $-0.2$). Extra Heavy completion delay $A=C_{\rm mixed}-C_{\rm isolated}$ is identical across all four settings within each seed ($3,0,7,1,8$ cycles; RR minus OF is $0$ in 10/10 comparisons). Ordering effects are quota-dependent: eligible RR lowers Q2 Sparse P99 by one cycle without an observed Q2 maximum-gap increase, but at Q1 it leaves Sparse P99 unchanged and raises the maximum Heavy gap by 1.6 cycles on average; this is not a universal ordering tradeoff.
 
